{
  "_about": [
    "Golden cases for what a device does when SubmitCommit refuses its commit, or fails without an answer.",
    "Normative: docs/delivery.md §A commit the server refuses.",
    "A lost answer: docs/delivery.md §SubmitCommit (A submit whose answer was lost).",
    "`verdicts`: one refusal each. `code` is the Connect code in its wire spelling. `localEpoch` is this device's epoch after clearing the refused commit; `headEpoch` the server's head epoch from FetchConversationHead, or null for no head; `verifierMatches` whether SHA-256 of this device's send key equals the head's verifier, or null when the head carried none. `headPassedThrough` (absent: false) is whether the head, below this device's epoch, is one its copy came through (`head_rolled_back` in mls/core): the group's own head once stood above it on this copy (a head read naming the copy there, a seat at such a head, or a merge of the device's own commit, never a commit merely placed for it), it names the send key the copy had there when that epoch is among the copy's last 64, and below them the copy has kept 64 epochs since its seat and opened a stamp since then, every stamp it opened named no steward key, and it counts and keeps none. `verdict` is `retry`, `behind`, `forked` or `rolledBack` (keep the copy: neither forget the group nor ask back in).",
    "Only `checkedCodes` are worth reading the head for; any other code is `retry`.",
    "`commitsTaken`: a submit that failed without an answer, and whether the server took it anyway. `commitEpoch` is the epoch the commit was built at, `headEpoch` the head's afterwards (null for none), and `headVerifierIsNext` whether the head's verifier is the one this commit would have set (null when the head carried none). `taken` true means merge the staged commit instead of clearing it.",
    "`stuckBehind`: one group's `behind` verdicts in order, each at `atMs` with this device's `localEpoch`; `stuck` is whether that verdict strands the device. A `clear` event resets the count, as any other verdict or a rejoin does. `lastDrainStartedAtMs`, when present, is when the last complete mailbox drain began. `stuckBehindMs`, `stuckBehindChecks` and `stuckBehindWindowMs` are the constants.",
    "`forkRejoinDelays`: how long a group rejoined over a fork waits before another fork may rejoin it, by rejoins so far. `forkRejoins`: one group's events in order; `rejoinedAt` records a rejoin and `checkAt` asks whether one may go ahead then (`may`). `forkRejoinFirstMs` and `forkRejoinCapMs` are the constants.",
    "To replay: run each case through your own implementation of the format and compare the result with the case's expectation, field by field."
  ],
